\f2 \cf2 \cb3 Definitions and notation: \cb1 \
\
\cb3 Suppose that the function $f: \\mathbb\{Z\}^\{+\} \\rightarrow \\mathbb\{Z\}^\{+\}$ is such that $$\\sum_\{n \\leq x\} f(n) \\sim C_f \\cdot x^R,$$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$ for some fixed, absolute real constants $C_f, R > 0$. Suppose that the sequence $\\mathcal\{F\} := \\\{f(n)\\\}_\{n \\geq 1\}$ denotes the set of distinct values of $f$ without multiplicity. Suppose that $\\mathcal\{F\}$ has the limiting asymptotic density $$A := \\lim_\{x \\rightarrow \\infty\} \\frac\{1\}\{x\} \\cdot \\#\\\{\\tilde\{f\} \\in \\mathcal\{F\}: \\tilde\{f\} \\leq x\\\}$$ for some fixed $A \\in (0, 1)$. Define the mean of the scaled function $f$ to be $\\mu_f(x) := \\frac\{1\}\{x\} \\cdot \\sum_\{n \\leq x\} f(n) n^\{-R\}$ where the function $x^\{R\} \\mu_f(x) > 0$ is a strictly increasing monotone function of $x$, i.e., so that $x^\{R\} \\mu_f(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$. For real $z > 0$, we define $$\\mathcal\{F\}_x(z) := \\frac\{1\}\{x\} \\cdot \\#\\\{n \\leq x: f(n) n^\{-R\} \\leq z\\\}$$. \cb1 \
\
\cb3 Problem statement:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 For this problem, we require the exact value of the following limit when $C_f := 3$ and $A := \\frac\{3\}\{\\pi^2\}$: $$L := \\lim_\{\\substack\{x \\rightarrow \\infty \\\\ z \\rightarrow \\infty\}\} \\mathcal\{F\}_x(z)\\,$$ where the limit is taken as $x$ and $z$ independently tend to positive infinity.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Answer format: \cb1 \
\
\cb3 Express the limit $L$ as an exact real fraction using well-known, standard real constants such as $\\pi$, $e$ or simplified radicals. \cb1 \
\
\cb3 Answer:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 $$L = \\frac\{3 e^\{-3\}\}\{2 \\sqrt\{6 \\pi^5\}\}.$$\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Supplementary documents:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 An attached PDF copy of the reference by Montgomery and Vaughan because it is sometimes hard to locate online free of charge. This copy of the book is copyrighted and should not be included with the dataset returned to the client. The attachment is meant only for the convenience of the reviewer.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Solution:\
\
Since $z \\rightarrow \\infty$, we can replace $z \\maptso N$ for integers $N \\geq 1$ with $N := \\lceil z \\rceil$ to compute the same limit $L$ as $z \\rightarrow \\infty$. It follows by adopting several long, but sufficiently detailed proof in Section 7.4 of the reference [MV] that $$\\mathcal\{F\}_x(N) \\sim \\sum_\{1 \\leq m \\leq N\} \\frac\{A \\cdot \\mu_f(x)^\{m-1\}\}\{(m-1)!\}.$$ We observe that $\\mu_f(x) \\sim C_f$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$ so that the latter sum may be expressed and accurately approximated by asymptotics of the (upper) incomplete gamma function $$\\Gamma(a, z) := \\int_\{z\}^\{\\infty\} t^\{a-1\} e^\{-t\} dt,$$ for positive real parameters $a$ and $z$ (see the appendix section in the reference [MDS]). Then we see that $$\\lim_\{N \\rightarrow \\infty\} \\mathcal\{F\}_x(N) \\sim \\sum_\{1 \\leq m \\leq \\mu_f(x)\} \\frac\{A \\cdot \\mu_f(x)^\{m-1\}\}\{(m-1)!\} \\sim \\frac\{A e^\{-C_f\}\}\{2 \\sqrt\{2 \\pi C_f\}\},$$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$. All that remains to compute the exact value of the finite real limit $L$ is to substitute and simplify. QED.\
\
References:\
\
[MV]: H.~L. Montgomery and R.~C. Vaughan. Multiplicative Number Theory: I, Classical Theory. Cambridge, 2006.\
[MDS]: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.05842"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \cb3 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.05842}} (see Appendix B)\cb1 \
